  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-6 tracking-tight">
      How Many Cinder Blocks for a Retaining Wall? (Formula &amp; Sizing Chart)
    </h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5 leading-relaxed">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Calculating the number of concrete blocks (CMU) needed for a retaining wall involves more than just counting rectangular blocks. Because standard cinder blocks are laid with a 3/8-inch bed and head mortar joint, their modular geometry simplifies into a universal multiplier: <strong>1.125 blocks per square foot</strong> of wall elevation.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Calculate Your Exact CMU Blocks &amp; Rebar Instantly</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4 text-sm">Enter your wall length and height to get exact CMU block counts, Type S mortar bags, core grout volume in cubic yards, and rebar steel schedule.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/concrete-block-cmu" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-2.5 px-5 rounded-lg text-sm transition-colors shadow-xs">
          Open Concrete Block (CMU) Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Standard CMU Block Formula</h2>
      <p>
        A standard American hollow CMU block has nominal dimensions of <strong>8" × 8" × 16"</strong>. Its actual physical dimensions are 7-5/8" × 7-5/8" × 15-5/8". With a standard 3/8" mortar joint, each block occupies exactly 128 square inches (0.888 sq ft) of face area.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm my-4 text-center">
        Total Blocks = Wall Length (ft) × Wall Height (ft) × 1.125 × 1.10 (Waste Buffer)
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Quick Reference Estimation Chart (8x8x16 Blocks)</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="min-w-full text-xs text-left border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
          <thead class="bg-gray-100 font-bold text-gray-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Wall Dimensions</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Surface Area</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Net CMU Blocks</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Blocks (+10% Waste)</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b">Mortar Bags (80 lb)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700">
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">10 ft × 3 ft</td><td class="p-3">30 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">34 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">38 blocks</td><td class="p-3">2 bags</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">20 ft × 3 ft</td><td class="p-3">60 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">68 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">75 blocks</td><td class="p-3">3 bags</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">25 ft × 4 ft</td><td class="p-3">100 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">113 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">125 blocks</td><td class="p-3">4 bags</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">30 ft × 4 ft</td><td class="p-3">120 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">135 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">150 blocks</td><td class="p-3">5 bags</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">40 ft × 6 ft</td><td class="p-3">240 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">270 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">297 blocks</td><td class="p-3">10 bags</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">50 ft × 6 ft</td><td class="p-3">300 sq ft</td><td class="p-3">338 blocks</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">372 blocks</td><td class="p-3">12 bags</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Crucial Structural Requirements for Retaining Walls</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>
          <strong>Concrete Footing Depth:</strong> Per <a href="/calculators/concrete-footing" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">IRC Section R403</a>, the poured concrete footing must sit below your regional frost line and be at least 16" wide and 10" thick with two continuous #4 steel rebar runs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vertical Rebar Cores:</strong> Place #4 or #5 vertical rebar every 16 to 32 inches on-center. Lap-splice these bars to the footing dowels by at least 24 inches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Core Grouting:</strong> Pour high-slump structural grout into every rebar cell. Solidly grouting an 8-inch CMU block wall requires roughly <strong>0.0074 cubic yards of grout per block</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hydrostatic Drainage:</strong> Backfill a 12-inch wide vertical column of clean #57 crushed stone behind the block wall, wrapped in geotextile filter fabric, with a perforated 4-inch drain pipe at the base.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Related Workflow CTA -->
      <div class="mt-8 p-6 bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-xl">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-300 mb-2">Complete Retaining Wall Workflow</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm mb-4">Follow our step-by-step 4-phase project workflow for excavation, footing pouring, block stacking, and drainage backfill.</p>
